@@ -69,6 +69,28 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json` method pretty prints a string containing JSON, with syntax highlighting. The output is valid JSON, indented by 4 spaces by default. Pass ``indent`` to change the number of spaces, or ``highlight=False`` to disable highlighting::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+The JSON is rendered by the :class:`~rich.json.JSON` renderable, which you can also print or log directly::
+
+    >>> from rich.json import JSON
+    >>> console.print(JSON('{"name": "Rich"}'))
+    >>> console.log(JSON('{"name": "Rich"}'))
+
+Output from JSON is never wrapped or cropped, so long lines are preserved.
+
+If you don't need a Console instance, import the :func:`~rich.print_json` function from the package::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('{"name": "Rich"}')
+
+A JSON file can be pretty printed from the command line with ``python -m rich.json PATH``.
+
